# Power limits for the Ryzen 7 8745HS (Geekom A8), applied by ryzen-tdp.service.
# Installed to /etc/default/ryzen-tdp by apply.sh, which refuses to touch any
# machine that is not that exact CPU in that exact chassis -- these are numbers
# for one thermal design, not a setting with a sensible general value.
#
# The firmware default is 45W sustained; the chip's configurable range is
# 35-54W. Values are milliwatts:
#
#   --stapm-limit  sustained (STAPM), the number that matters for long loads
#   --slow-limit   PPT slow, kept equal to STAPM
#   --fast-limit   PPT fast, the short-burst ceiling, set a little above
#
# No --tctl-temp on purpose: the firmware setpoint is 92C and raising it trades
# margin for a couple of percent. First measured at 52W under a 90s all-core
# load: 11379 bogo ops/s, 4474 MHz steady, 88.5C peak, and PPT SLOW drawing its
# full 52.00 -- power-limited, with headroom. That was one room on one day
# though, and an earlier session measured the same chip pinning at 92C and
# pulling back to 53W when asked for 54W. If it starts pinning under load,
# lower these rather than raising tctl: the chassis is the constraint here, not
# the silicon.
#
# It started pinning, so 52W -> 50W. thermal-log caught a 49-minute all-core
# load on 2026-10-02 holding Tctl at exactly 92.0C for ~40 minutes, drawing
# 51.3W on average against the 52W asked for, at 4432 MHz -- 40-60 MHz under
# the unpinned figure. The thermal limit, not the power limit, was setting the
# clock, so the last two watts bought heat and nothing else. The burst limit is
# untouched: 58W is a ceiling for seconds, not a sustained draw.
#
# The burst limit was then measured with burst-bench.py on 2026-10-03, three
# shuffled runs per limit, each from idle. A ~5s all-core burst took 5.165s at
# 50W (no burst at all: PPT fast equal to sustained), 5.079s at 54W, 5.000s at
# 58W and 4.943s at 62W, peaking at 74/76/80/82C. Each watt bought ~0.4% up to
# 58W and ~0.3% past it. On a ~12s burst 62W's lead was gone: 11.949s against
# 11.924s at 58W, inside 58W's own run-to-run range, for 1.4W more and a ~2C
# hotter peak. A higher fast limit spends the PPT slow budget sooner, so 62W
# stops bursting ~2s before 58W does and gives its lead back. 58W stays: past
# it the extra watts buy ~60ms on a 5s load and nothing measurable on a 12s one.
RYZENADJ_ARGS="--stapm-limit=50000 --slow-limit=50000 --fast-limit=58000"
